LLM API Error Handling Reference Notes
A comprehensive guide to identifying, understanding, and mitigating the six most common code-level API errors encountered when developing robust LLM and AI applications.

Quick Reference: Common LLM API Errors
Error Code & Type	Primary Cause	Recommended Solution / Mitigation
Rate Limits (429)	Sending too many requests in a short time.	Implement exponential backoff with jitter; reduce request frequency; spread load across keys or upgrade tier.
Timeout (504)	Processing took longer than the allowed time limit.	Reduce input payload size; simplify prompts; increase timeout and max_retries parameters in LangChain.
Invalid Requests (400, 413)	The request is malformed, missing fields, or exceeds size limits.	Validate and sanitize all incoming payloads prior to transmission (e.g., using Pydantic models).
Content Policy (400)	Input or output violates the provider's safety/moderation rules.	Review provider policies, filter input text before submission, and adjust prompt wording.
Quota Exceeded (429)	Account usage limit reached or credits ran out.	Upgrade plan, configure auto-recharge, and establish real-time usage/spending alerts.
Server Overload (503, 529)	The provider's servers are busy, overwhelmed, or experiencing downtime.	Check the provider status page, retry with backoff, or degrade gracefully with fallback providers/responses.
Detailed Breakdown & Mitigation Strategies
1. Rate Limits (429)
What it means: Your application is firing requests too rapidly, breaching the provider's threshold for your current tier.

Mitigation Strategies:

Exponential Backoff & Jitter: Automatically pause and retry failed requests with progressively longer waiting periods randomized with jitter to prevent simultaneous retry spikes.

Load Management: Spread your request volume across multiple API keys or throttle frequency.

Scaling: Upgrade to a higher subscription tier if hitting caps consistently during normal business operations.

2. Timeout (504)
What it means: The model took too long to generate the response or handle the input context window before the connection dropped.

Mitigation Strategies:

Payload Optimization: Reduce input sizes, truncate unnecessary history, or simplify complex multi-step prompts.

Parameter Adjustment: Explicitly raise timeout thresholds using the timeout and max_retries configurations within LangChain.

3. Invalid Requests (400, 413)
What it means: The API rejected the request because fields were missing, malformed, or the payload size exceeded limits.

Mitigation Strategies:

Pre-Flight Validation: Use validation frameworks (like Pydantic) to inspect and reject malformed schemas before spending tokens or hitting the API network layer.

4. Content Policy (400)
What it means: The prompt text or generated output triggered the provider's safety, privacy, or content moderation guardrails.

Mitigation Strategies:

Content Filtering: Review provider constraints, sanitize inputs, and strip out potentially triggering keywords or unsafe phrases prior to submission.

5. Quota Exceeded (429)
What it means: Your organization has depleted its pre-paid credits or monthly billing cap.

Mitigation Strategies:

Billing Automation: Set up auto-recharge settings and proactive usage alerts.

Budget Management: Monitor token usage per request and enforce user-level rate limits or spending caps.

6. Server Overload (503, 529)
What it means: The LLM provider's infrastructure is experiencing high traffic or an active outage.

Mitigation Strategies:

Graceful Degradation: Wrap critical LLM execution calls inside try/except blocks to catch outages cleanly. Provide user-friendly fallback messaging, switch to a smaller backup model, or route requests to a secondary fallback provider.
